    "imgAlt": "‘Mr Lucky’ on a Mission to Ensure His Winning Streak Is No Fluke",
    "cap": "Rejesh Satiija, pictured with his family.",
    "contentHtml": "<p><strong>Rejesh Satiija</strong> is a familiar name among prime ministers and presidents, among whom he commands respect for the way he conducts himself and his businesses. His Sun &amp; Sand Group of Companies has an international presence, with businesses spanning mines, metals, real estate, agriculture, farming and hubs in London, Dubai and Nigeria.</p>\n<p>Born in north India to a school teacher mother and a father who was a government employee, <strong>Satiija</strong> grew up in a home where the emphasis was on values and faith. He qualified as a chartered engineer and initially worked for a local procurement authority, before realising his destiny lay elsewhere. He left India for Nigeria in 1993 with no more than a hunch that there was business to be done.</p>\n<blockquote>“It all depends on how you treat the other person”<cite>— <strong>Rejesh Satiija</strong></cite></blockquote>\n<p>His first break was in mining, producing aluminium alloys that could be used as car parts — his clients, Toyota and Rolls Royce, had never before sourced parts from northern Africa. It was a major coup and <strong>Satiija</strong>'s status in Nigeria rose as a consequence.</p>\n<p>“We are the pioneers in Nigeria, and the first engineering group from the African continent to export our products to companies with advanced technology and engineering — to companies such as Aconix, Nissan, Sundaram Clayton,” he told the GG2 Power 101.</p>\n<p>One of the more recent acquisitions in <strong>Satiija</strong>'s empire is 5,000 acres of land in Canada and Georgia for the cultivation of apples and cherries. He is also building a network of cold storage facilities in India to assist with the import of apples, and aims to be the world's largest importer of the fruit by 2018.</p>\n<p>Outside business, <strong>Satiija</strong> is also increasingly involved in philanthropic activities. One of these is his chairmanship of the Indian Council for UN Relations. It is a group dedicated towards furthering UN goals and encourages member states to discuss issues and broker possible solutions.</p>\n<p><strong>Satiija</strong> hopes to be able to bridge the widening gap between the rich and the poor, as he believes India's best assets are its young population and entrepreneurs. “Criticism is not a solution. The solution is to make an effort,” he said.</p>",
